    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "When a new owner is created, the app now saves `namesakeCount`: the number of owners who already existed with the same first and last name, ignoring case. `GET /api/owners/{id}` returns it. The test suite passes: `AcceptanceTest.coreCountsNamesakes`, the only test, ran green.\n\n**How it works:**\n- **Where it's set:** `ClinicServiceImpl.saveOwner` sets the count only when the owner is new, just before saving, using the same pattern as `customerCode`. That means the new owner is never counted, and updating an owner leaves the value alone.\n- **Database:** I added a `namesake_count INTEGER` column to the `owners` table in all four schema files (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres), and a matching `namesakeCount` field to the `Owner` entity.\n- **Repository:** I added `countByFirstNameAndLastNameIgnoringCase` to `OwnerRepository`, with versions for all three data-access setups (Spring Data JPA, JPA and JDBC). It lowercases both names and compares them. The JDBC version's queries also read and update the new column.\n- **API:** `openapi.yml` now lists `namesakeCount` as a read-only integer on `Owner`, so the generated `OwnerDto` includes it. `OwnerMapper` skips it when building an `Owner` from request data.\n\nThe tests only run the default setup (H2 database, Spring Data JPA), so the JPA and JDBC versions compile but no test exercises them. Owners that existed before this change will have no stored value, so they'll come back with `namesakeCount: null`.",
